**Read:** [Notes on Computational Fluid Dynamics — Greenshields and Weller](../sources/Notes-on-CFD-Greenshields-Weller.lingua#r_50W8G5SX00YH7R6TBXK4FAP5MY), advection, boundedness and discretization errors; [Computational Fluid Dynamics: An Open-Source Approach — Vermeire and colleagues](../sources/Computational-Fluid-Dynamics-Vermeire-Pereira-Karbasian.lingua#r_7B08DEGENZD4JFFRFNPKJCVFCW), numerical schemes and verification; [Fluid Mechanics — David Tong](../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_3SP698XHQFSZZ6N9Q22NESHMEA), chapter 4 conservation, characteristics and shocks.

Upwinding uses propagation direction to construct a stable transport approximation but introduces numerical diffusion. Higher-order reconstruction can reduce smooth-region error and still generate oscillations near discontinuities unless limited appropriately. State whether an accuracy claim concerns smooth fields, cell averages or a norm that includes a moving shock.

Derive the modified equation or Fourier amplification factor for a simple linear scheme. For nonlinear conservation laws, compare a central dissipative flux with an approximate Riemann flux and identify their wave-speed bounds. Check positivity of density, depth or concentration in the actual update; clipping a negative result after the fact can destroy the conservation account.

Test smooth periodic transport, a sharp transported profile and a shock/rarefaction problem with an independent solution. Vary time step as well as mesh. Report front location, integral error, overshoot and diffusion, and explain why a visually smooth result may reflect excessive numerical damping rather than correctly resolved physics.

